    {
      "id": "evt_gcal_com_effective_altruism_nyc_d9922e74",
      "name": "EA NYC Beginner-Friendly Summer Hike #2!",
      "type": "effective altruism",
      "locationId": "",
      "communityId": "com_effective_altruism_nyc",
      "description": "(The following description is a draft subject to change: check this page again before the event, which will be either Sat Sep 12 or Sun Sep 13.)\n\nTime for another relaxed stroll through the woods!  We'll be walking the Pine Meadow Trail in Harriman State Park, stopping to eat a packed lunch by the stream.  Key points follow, more details below.\nKey points - read this!\nThe hike is currently scheduled for Sat Sep 12, but we could move it to Sun Sep 13 depending on weather.\nMeet me (Jacob Eliosoff) at Penn Station at 8:45 am, in the NJ Transit area (31st St & 7th Ave).  We'll take the 9:14 am train to Secaucus, and connect there to the Port Jervis line train to Sloatsburg, arriving at 10:24 am.  (Buy your round trip ticket here, $30.)  You must catch that 9:14 am train or you miss the hike!I'll keep an eye on jacob.eliosoff@gmail.com/FB Messenger up to 9:30 am, but may be hard to reach during the hike.\nBring: water, a packed lunch (no restos on the trail)/snacks, comfy shoes + socks, ideally a hat.  I recommend long (light) pants rather than shorts, to avoid scratches & ticks.\nWe'll finish by returning from Sloatsburg on the 4:25 pm train arriving back at Penn at 5:36 pm.\nOverall itinerary:\n8:45 am: meet in Penn Station, NJ Transit area.\n9:14 am: train to Secaucus leaves.\n9:30 am: connecting train from Secaucus to Sloatsburg leaves.\n10:24 am: train arrives at Sloatsburg.\nHike along Harriman State Park's Pine Meadow Trail.Total mileage is 6-8 non-steep miles.\n4:25 pm: catch the Port Jervis Line train at Sloatsburg.\n5:36 pm: train arrives at Penn Station.\nOther notes:\nAll are welcome!  We'll go pretty slowly, with breaks.  This route has no extended climbs/descents.\nNonprofit workers and students are eligible for a discount on the bus/train tickets - contact me.\nTicks are always a risk in NY nature, as some of us learned a few hikes ago.  They're harmless if removed within a day: I suggest giving yourself a close body inspection once you get home after the hike.",
      "startDate": "2026-09-12T08:25:00-04:00",
      "endDate": "2026-09-12T17:05:00-04:00",
